{"timestamp":"2026-10-03T12:55:09.934Z","ordinal":17199,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a101d5-2b6e-7b71-883d-179d0834d7fc","author":"/root/astra_ultra_final_judge","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/astra_ultra_final_judge\nPayload:\nThe priority is reasonable **as an investigation order**, with three caveats:\n\n- CAD’s large inventory makes it the strongest next **cost-attribution target**, not yet a proven bottleneck. Confirm its actual draws, selected LOD and vertex/fragment costs in the existing capture before changing geometry.\n- Hidden-part removal needs enclosure proof across articulation, camera angles and lighting—including shadows, AO and reflections. Pixel invisibility at one pose is insufficient. Exact reuse must preserve normals, UVs and tangent discontinuities.\n- CAD processing in shadow/AO passes overlaps those categories; avoid counting the potential saving twice.\n\nLowering whole-cell occlusion priority is supported by the two sampled views. It does not rule out finer occlusion elsewhere. Ground/dust remain measured costs, but previously unsuccessful shader variants should not regain priority without new attribution."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a101c3-e32d-7580-ba72-be0d3d17ace2","create_time":1791032109.934102}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-03T12:55:32.136Z","ordinal":17200,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a0fd98-2f5c-78c3-9769-86fbf8a6fc22","turn_id":"01a101c3-e32d-7580-ba72-be0d3d17ace2","item":{"type":"AgentMessage","id":"msg_08092949d98b672f016ac0fb30785087d29d66daffdbb0b448","content":[{"type":"Text","text":"**Yes—geometry is a strong suspect, especially Marvin itself. But the GPU also spends substantial time on shading and repeated rendering passes.**\n\nThe latest native scene audit found:\n\n| Geometry inside the camera’s viewing volume | Base triangles |\n|---|---:|\n| Marvin and its attached geometry | **992,079** |\n| Crowd batches | 117,147 |\n| Town architecture | 109,424 |\n\nThese are mesh counts, **not confirmed GPU submissions**. Nevertheless, Marvin deserves investigation before reducing town detail.\n\nMy optimization plan, in priority order:\n\n1. **Attribute Marvin’s actual GPU cost.** Identify its draws in Xcode’s Metal capture, including shadow and ambient-occlusion passes. Investigate enclosed motors, electronics and internal surfaces. Remove geometry only when it cannot affect appearance across camera angles, articulation, shadows and reflections. Preserve exterior detail.\n\n2. **Reduce repeated geometry processing.** The earlier capture processed approximately 1.23 million primitives in both the main and ambient-occlusion preparation passes, plus roughly 1.7 million in each shadow pass. Investigate shared geometry, tighter shadow-caster selection and reusable static work.\n\n3. **Improve visibility filtering where measurements justify it.** We already spatially batch the town. At two tested street viewpoints, every in-frustum architecture cell contributed pixels; simply hiding whole cells would damage the image. Investigate smaller batches and conservative occlusion behind buildings, while retaining off-screen objects whose shadows remain visible.\n\n4. **Optimize ground, dust and ambient-occlusion shading.** These have measured GPU costs. Reduce redundant calculations, texture reads and transparent coverage while preserving the rendered result. Revisit previous unsuccessful experiments only with new evidence.\n\n5. **Check GPU scheduling and resource traffic.** Investigate unnecessary uploads, mipmap generation and dependencies between passes. Spare CPU cores cannot compensate for GPU work that misses the frame deadline.\n\nEach change gets a controlled comparison, image review and a measured GPU result. Final acceptance remains **ten-minute clear and storm drives on this M2**, full detail, actual presentation timing, and a target GPU P99 below **12.5 ms** for headroom.\n\nThe Astra Ultra judge reviewed this order and agreed—with the explicit caveat that Marvin’s large mesh count is an investigation target, not yet proof of the bottleneck. **Constant 60 FPS is still unproven.**"}],"phase":"final_answer"},"started_at_ms":1791032112514,"completed_at_ms":1791032132136}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-03T12:55:32.143Z","ordinal":17201,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","id":"msg_08092949d98b672f016ac0fb30785087d29d66daffdbb0b448","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"**Yes—geometry is a strong suspect, especially Marvin itself. But the GPU also spends substantial time on shading and repeated rendering passes.**\n\nThe latest native scene audit found:\n\n| Geometry inside the camera’s viewing volume | Base triangles |\n|---|---:|\n| Marvin and its attached geometry | **992,079** |\n| Crowd batches | 117,147 |\n| Town architecture | 109,424 |\n\nThese are mesh counts, **not confirmed GPU submissions**. Nevertheless, Marvin deserves investigation before reducing town detail.\n\nMy optimization plan, in priority order:\n\n1. **Attribute Marvin’s actual GPU cost.** Identify its draws in Xcode’s Metal capture, including shadow and ambient-occlusion passes. Investigate enclosed motors, electronics and internal surfaces. Remove geometry only when it cannot affect appearance across camera angles, articulation, shadows and reflections. Preserve exterior detail.\n\n2. **Reduce repeated geometry processing.** The earlier capture processed approximately 1.23 million primitives in both the main and ambient-occlusion preparation passes, plus roughly 1.7 million in each shadow pass. Investigate shared geometry, tighter shadow-caster selection and reusable static work.\n\n3. **Improve visibility filtering where measurements justify it.** We already spatially batch the town. At two tested street viewpoints, every in-frustum architecture cell contributed pixels; simply hiding whole cells would damage the image. Investigate smaller batches and conservative occlusion behind buildings, while retaining off-screen objects whose shadows remain visible.\n\n4. **Optimize ground, dust and ambient-occlusion shading.** These have measured GPU costs. Reduce redundant calculations, texture reads and transparent coverage while preserving the rendered result. Revisit previous unsuccessful experiments only with new evidence.\n\n5. **Check GPU scheduling and resource traffic.** Investigate unnecessary uploads, mipmap generation and dependencies between passes. Spare CPU cores cannot compensate for GPU work that misses the frame deadline.\n\nEach change gets a controlled comparison, image review and a measured GPU result. Final acceptance remains **ten-minute clear and storm drives on this M2**, full detail, actual presentation timing, and a target GPU P99 below **12.5 ms** for headroom.\n\nThe Astra Ultra judge reviewed this order and agreed—with the explicit caveat that Marvin’s large mesh count is an investigation target, not yet proof of the bottleneck. **Constant 60 FPS is still unproven.**"}],"phase":"final_answer","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a101c3-e32d-7580-ba72-be0d3d17ace2","content_item_kinds":["unknown"]}}}
